
/2E    P C / M - 1 2   S P L              PAL8-V12B 04-SEP-87 PAGE 1


             / DIGITAL I/O ROUTINES

             /THIS CODE IMPLEMENTS THE '@' COMMAND AND THE 'FDIN' FUNC-
             /TION FOR SETTING AND READING THE 12310 12-BIT DIGITAL I/O
             /BOARD.  THE '@' COMMAND ALLOWS INDIVIDUAL BITS TO BE SET,
             /EITHER AS A GROUP, OR SEQUENTIALLY, WHICH ALLOWS A SERIES
             /OF PULSES TO BE GENERATED, WITH THE SPACING DETERMINED BY
             /SPL'S EXECUTION TIME.

             /THE 'FDIN' FUNCTION READS THE INPUT BUFFER THROUGH A MASK
             /SET UP BY THE FUNCTION ARGUMENTS. THE '@' COMMAND AND THE
             /'FDIN' FUNCTION USE SIMILAR MASKS: BITS ARE NUMBERED FROM
             /1-12 WITH '1' BEING THE MSB (THE BIT USUALLY LABELED '0')
             /AND '12' BEING THE LSB (THE BIT USUALLY LABELED '11').

             /THE REASON FOR THIS CONVENTION IS THAT '0' THEN REFERS TO
             /-ALL- BITS, I.E. '@ 0' CLEARS -ALL- OUTPUTS AND 'FDIN(0)'
             /READS -ALL- INPUTS.  (THE '0' MAY BE OMITTED, I.E. '@' OR
             /'FDIN()' IS ENOUGH.)  INDIVIDUAL BITS ARE 'ENABLED' BY A
             /POSITIVE NUMBER.  THUS '@ 1,2' TURNS THOSE TWO BITS ON,
             /WHILE '@-1,-2' TURNS THEM OFF AGAIN -- ALL OTHER BITS ARE
             /UNAFFECTED BY THIS COMMAND.  THE BITS ARE CHANGED SEQUEN-
             /TIALLY AS THE COMMAND IS DECODED, SO BIT 1 WILL BE TURNED
             /OFF BEFORE BIT 2.  IN ORDER TO SET (AND/OR CLEAR) SEVERAL
             /BITS SIMULTANEOUSLY, THEY SHOULD BE GROUPED BY ENCLOSING
             /THEM IN PARENTHESES.  FOR EXAMPLE:

             /'@,1,(2,-1),<3,4,5>,-5,-2' WILL FIRST CLEAR ALL BITS ('0'
             /IS IMPLIED BY THE MISSING FIRST ARGUMENT), THEN SET BIT 1,
             /THEN SIMULTANEOUSLY CLEAR IT & SET BIT 2 AFTER A DELAY OF
             /SEVERAL MILLISECONDS, THEN TURN ON BITS 3,4,5 ALL AT ONCE,
             /THEN TURN OFF BIT 5 AND FINALLY CLEAR BIT 2, LEAVING BITS
             /3-4 STILL ON.  NESTED PARENTHESES ARE ILLEGAL, AND 'OFF'
             /SUPERCEDES 'ON', MAKING '@(-1,1)' THE SAME AS '@-1'.  TO
             /LENGTHEN PULSES, JUST SET AND CLEAR BIT 13 A FEW TIMES.

             /THE '=' OPTION LETS COMMANDS OF THE FORM '@=N' LOAD THE 4
             /LOW-ORDER BITS AS A SINGLE NUMBER, I.E. '@=5' SETS BITS 9
             /AND 11, CLEARS 8 AND 10.  BITS 0-7 WILL NOT BE DISTURBED.
             /THIS OPTION ALLOWS A SINGLE ARITHMETIC EXPRESSION TO PRO-
             /GRAM THE LAST FOUR BITS ALL AT ONCE.  THE '=' OPTION CAN
             /BE MIXED WITH OTHER BIT OPERATIONS IN A SINGLE COMMAND,
             /BUT IT CANNOT APPEAR INSIDE PARENTHESES.

             /'FDIN(+3)' ONLY READS BIT '3' OF THE INPUT BUFFER, WHILE
             /'FDIN(-3)' READS EVERYTHING -BUT- BIT 3.  (NEGATIVE ARGU-
             /MENTS HAVE A DIFFERENT MEANING IN THE '@' COMMAND.)  THIS
             /SCHEME ALLOWS A VERY FLEXIBLE BIT MASK TO BE CONSTRUCTED:
             /'FDIN(-1,-3)' READS EVERYTHING EXCEPT BITS 1 $ 3.  (NOTE:
             /ONLY EDGE-SENSITIVE BITS WHICH ARE READ WILL BE CLEARED.)

